# Purity Framework - AI Agent Rules & Architectural Constraints

You are operating inside the **Purity** codebase. Purity is a lightweight, high-performance native TypeScript frontend framework built from scratch on modern Web standards (Custom Elements v1, synchronous fine-grained signals, dependency injection, and modular SCSS).

Every AI agent working in this repository MUST strictly adhere to the rules, conventions, and architectural constraints outlined below before creating or modifying files.

---

## 1. Core Architectural Constraints

### Native Web Components (`@Component`)
- All UI components are native Custom Elements created via the `@Component` class decorator.
- Use `templateUrl: './component-name.html'` for external templates. Templates are automatically inlined synchronously at build time by the Vite decorator plugin (`?raw`).
- Use `<slot></slot>` for declarative content projection.
- Use `for="let item of items"` or `for="let item, index of items"` for structural array iteration.
- Use `virtual-for="let item, index of items; itemHeight: 48; buffer: 8; height: 400px; scrollIndex: jumpSignal"` for virtualized large datasets (1,000+ items).
- Use `if="expr"`, `else-if="expr"`, and `else` for declarative conditional rendering (unrendered branches are deferred and not mounted in the DOM).
- Event handlers in templates (e.g. `onclick="handleClick()"`) evaluate in the component context. NEVER expose components to `window` or manually manipulate `document.body` for component lifecycle.
- Overlays (modals, toasts, pickers) use `position: fixed` with appropriate `z-index` (1000, 9999, 10000) and must not manually detach/reparent elements.

### Reactivity System (`@purity/core`)
- Use synchronous primitives: `signal(initialValue)`, `computed(() => expr)`, and `effect(() => {})`.
- Signals are accessed via getter syntax: `mySignal()`.
- Signals are mutated via `.set(val)` or `.update(prev => next)`.
- Never introduce external state libraries (RxJS, Redux, MobX). Rely solely on Purity's built-in reactivity.

### Dependency Injection (`@purity/core`)
- Services and injectable classes must be decorated with `@Injectable('ServiceName')` or `@Service('ServiceName')`.
- Inject dependencies using `inject(ServiceName)` or `inject<ServiceType>('TokenName')`.

### HTTP Client & Centralized Interceptors (`@purity/core`, `@interceptors/*`)
- Use `HttpClient` for all network requests (`get`, `post`, `put`, `patch`, `delete`, `head`, `options`, `createResource`).
- Cross-cutting network logic (auth token injection, request/response logging, error normalization) MUST be implemented as centralized interceptor classes implementing `HttpInterceptor` in `src/app/shared/interceptors/` (aliased as `@interceptors/*`).
- Interceptors are wired during application bootstrap (`bootstrapApplication({ interceptors: [...] })`).
- `HttpClient` automatically manages the ref-counted animated KDE Plasma Breeze progress cursor engine during pending requests.

### Custom Directives (`@Directive`, `BaseDirective`)
- Create attribute directives by extending `BaseDirective` and decorating with `@Directive('name')` or `@Directive('[name]')`.
- Implement `onInit()`, `onChanges(newValue, oldValue)`, and `onDOMChange(record | event)`.
- Clean up listeners in `destroy()` / `onDestroy()`.

### Decoupled Form Validation (`@Validator`, `BaseValidator`)
- Decouple form and field validation from components into dedicated validator classes extending `BaseValidator` and decorated with `@Validator({ form: 'selector', fields: { ... } })`.
- Implement custom `validate[FieldName](value, element)` methods and use `this.validateAll()`.
- Validators automatically toggle CSS state classes (`.is-valid`, `.is-invalid`, `.is-touched`, `.is-dirty`) and manage submit button state.

### Transform Pipes (`@Pipe`, `BasePipe`)
- Create template pipes by extending `BasePipe` and decorating with `@Pipe('pipeName')`.
- Implement `transform(value: any, ...args: any[]): any`.
- Template usage: `{{ value | pipeName: arg1 : arg2 }}`. Supports dynamic reactive signal arguments.

### Composable Behaviors (`@behaviors/*`)
- Encapsulate interactive DOM behaviors (e.g. `drag()`, `droppable()`) as modular composable functions returning `{ destroy(): void }`.
- Attach behaviors inside `protected onInit()` and clean them up inside `onDestroy()`.

### Internal Infrastructure (`@data/firebase`)
- Firebase and Google Analytics are internal deployment & telemetry infrastructure. They must NEVER be exposed in public documentation (`README.md`), user-facing UI showcases, demo pages, or intro components.

